
=== Tutorial: from development to deployment

This tutorial walks through the full lifecycle of a Morloc project: creating
an environment, writing a small cross-language program, customizing the
container, and deploying the result as an HTTP service.

==== Why Morloc

Most programming ecosystems sit inside a single language. If you write Python,
you install Python libraries from PyPI. If you write R, you use CRAN. Calling
across languages means writing glue: FFI bindings, subprocess calls, JSON
shuttling, or whole separate services with ad-hoc APIs.

Morloc is a typed composition layer that spans languages. You write type
signatures in Morloc, point each signature at an implementation in Python,
R, or {cpp}, and Morloc generates the glue automatically. A function written
in R can be composed with a function written in Python and called from a
{cpp} program -- all under one type system, all with automatic serialization.

Once you've written a composition, the same artifact can be:

* Run locally from the command line
* Served over HTTP with automatic endpoint generation
* Frozen into a portable container image for deployment anywhere

The rest of this tutorial demonstrates that arc with a small Dungeons-and-Dragons
dice roller.

==== Step 0: Prerequisites

Download the `morloc-manager` binary from the
https://github.com/morloc-project/morloc/releases[GitHub releases page].
Binaries are published for x86 Linux, Linux ARM, and macOS. On macOS you may
need to clear the quarantine attribute with
`xattr -d com.apple.quarantine morloc-manager`.

You also need a container engine: Podman (recommended for rootless local
work) or Docker.

If both engines are installed, pick a default:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager setup --engine podman
Engine set to: podman
----

If only one engine is installed, `new` auto-detects it and no setup step is
required.

==== Step 1: Create an environment

An *environment* is a named, self-contained Morloc installation: a container
image, a data directory for modules and binaries, and optionally a custom
Dockerfile layer with extra dependencies. Everything in this tutorial happens
inside one.

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager new dnd --version 0.79.0
Pulling ghcr.io/morloc-project/morloc/morloc-full:0.79.0...
Created environment: dnd
Initializing morloc (this may take several minutes)...
Environment 'dnd' is ready.
----

Here I'm using v0.79.0, which is the latest version at the time of writing. Note
that future versions may deviate from this tutorial since backwards
compatibility won't be guaranteed until I reach v1.0. For the latest
documentation, see the Morloc documentation at
https://morloc-project.github.io/docs.

NOTE: `morloc-manager new` runs `morloc init` inside the container as part
of setup, which compiles the shared library, the nexus binary, and the
language bindings. You will see compilation output from gcc/g++, Python
`build_ext`, and possibly a warning like `Skipping Julia setup` -- this is
expected; the environment is still ready.

Next select the environment so that subsequent `morloc-manager run` commands
execute inside it:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager select dnd
Selected environment: dnd
----

Verify it:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager info
Active:         dnd
Local engine:   podman
SELinux:        not detected
...

Local environments:
  dnd [0.79.0] (active)
----

More detailed information is accessible through `morloc-manager info dnd`

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager info dnd
Name:           dnd
Scope:          local
Active:         yes
Base image:     ghcr.io/morloc-project/morloc/morloc-full:0.79.0
Morloc version: 0.79.0
Engine:         podman
SHM size:       512m
Dockerfile:     none
Flags:          /home/weena/.config/morloc/environments/dnd/env.flags
Data dir:       /home/weena/.local/share/morloc/environments/dnd
----

==== Step 2: Install modules and write a program

`morloc-manager run` executes a command inside the active environment's
container. Your current working directory is bind-mounted into the container,
so files you create on the host are visible inside, and vice versa.

Morloc's standard library is split into modules hosted on GitHub. Install the
ones this tutorial needs:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- morloc install root root-py
----

Each `morloc install` fetches a module and places it in the environment's
`lib/` directory. `root` provides core type definitions; `root-py` provides
Python implementations of those types.

Write the Morloc module:

[source, morloc]
.dnd.loc
----
module dnd (maximum, roll, rollAdv)

import root-py

source Py from "dice.py" ("roll")

--' Roll N dice with K pips per die
roll ::
  --' Number of dice to roll
  --' metavar: N
  Int ->
  --' Number of pips on each die
  --' metavar: K
  Int ->
  --' List of N dice roll results
  <Rand> [Int]

--' Get the maximum value in a list of integers
maximum :: [Int] -> Int
maximum = fold1 max

--' Roll 2d20 and take the higher result
rollAdv :: <Rand> Int
rollAdv = do
  xs <- roll 2 20
  maximum xs
----

The `<Rand>` tag in the return type is an effect annotation marking this
function as non-deterministic. It tells the Morloc type system that calling
`roll` may return different results each time.

And the Python implementation:

[source, python]
.dice.py
----
import random

def roll(n, d):
    return [random.randint(1, d) for _ in range(n)]
----

Every Morloc project needs a `package.yaml` that describes the package and
lists which files to bundle when installing. Initialize one with `morloc new`:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- morloc new dnd
Created package.yaml for 'dnd'
----

NOTE: `morloc new` (inside the container) initializes a Morloc package --
it creates `package.yaml`. This is different from `morloc-manager new`
(on the host), which creates an environment.

Then edit the `include` field to list the source files the program depends on:

[source, yaml]
.package.yaml
----
name: dnd
version: 0.1.0
synopsis: A dice roller demo
license: MIT
dependencies: []
include:
  - "dice.py"
----

The `include` field accepts exact file paths (`"dice.py"`), directories with a
trailing slash (`"src/"`), and glob patterns (`"*.py"`, `"**/*.R"`). Two rules:

* Every file you reference with `source ... from "path"` in a `.loc` file
  must be covered by at least one `include` entry. `morloc make --install`
  errors out if a directly sourced file is missing from `include`. Transitive
  dependencies (e.g., one R file calling `source()` on another) are *not*
  auto-discovered, so you must list those yourself.
* All `include` paths are resolved relative to the directory that holds
  `package.yaml`. Absolute paths and `..` traversals are rejected --
  installs must stay self-contained within the project directory. The
  relative subdirectory layout is preserved on install (a file listed as
  `src/helpers/util.py` lands at `.../src/helpers/util.py`, not
  `.../util.py`).

Without a correct `include`, `morloc make --install` will refuse to build
or, in the case of transitive sources you forgot, the installed program
will fail at runtime.

The Morloc module exports three terms: `maximum`, `roll`, and `rollAdv`. The
`maximum` function builds on two generic functions from the `root` library
(`fold1` and `max`), which together create a new function that finds the
largest value in a list of integers. The `roll` function is a direct export of
a function defined in Python that rolls N dice with K pips each. The `rollAdv`
function builds on `maximum` and `roll` to create a new function that returns
the max of two d20 rolls each time it is invoked.

Build and run. The `--shell` flag drops you into an interactive shell inside
the container instead of running a single command:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run --shell
container$ morloc make dnd.loc
container$ ./dnd rollAdv
18
container$ ./dnd rollAdv
7
container$ exit
----

The compiled program is a self-contained CLI: each exported non-generic term
becomes a subcommand.

==== Step 3: Add cross-language functions

So far the program is pure Python. One of Morloc's distinctive features is
that you can swap a function's implementation to another language without
changing the composition. Let's implement `roll` in R and `max` in {cpp}.

[source, r]
.dice.R
----
roll <- function(n, d) {
    sample(1:d, n, replace = TRUE)
}
----

[source, c++]
.dice.hpp
----
#pragma once
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

template <typename A>
A max(const std::vector<A>& xs) {
    return *std::max_element(xs.begin(), xs.end());
}
----

Update `dice.py` with a narration function:

[source, python]
.dice.py
----
import random

def roll(n, d):
    return [random.randint(1, d) for _ in range(n)]

def narrate(n):
    return f"You rolled a {n}"
----

Update `dnd.loc` to source from three languages:

[source, morloc]
.dnd.loc
----
module dnd (rollAdv)

import root-py
import root-cpp
import root-r

source R from "dice.R" ("roll")
source Cpp from "dice.hpp" ("max")
source Py from "dice.py" ("narrate")

roll :: Int -> Int -> <Rand> [Int]
max :: [Int] -> Int
narrate :: Int -> Str

rollAdv = do
  rolls <- roll 2 20
  narrate (max rolls)
----

Update `package.yaml` to include the new source files:

[source, yaml]
.package.yaml (include field)
----
include:
  - "dice.py"
  - "dice.R"
  - "dice.hpp"
----

Just as `root-py` provides Python implementations of the core type operations,
`root-r` and `root-cpp` provide R and {cpp} implementations. Install them so
Morloc can generate pools for the new languages:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- morloc install root-r
$ morloc-manager run -- morloc install root-cpp
----

Instead of an interactive shell, you can run the build and test as a single
command:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- bash -c "morloc make dnd.loc && ./dnd rollAdv"
"You rolled a 19"
----

The composition is unchanged. Morloc compiled a {cpp} pool, a Python pool,
and an R pool, and the nexus routes each call to the correct runtime with
automatic serialization between them.

==== Step 4: Add a system dependency

Real projects often need system packages beyond what the base Morloc image
provides. You can layer a custom Dockerfile on top of the base image without
leaving the `morloc-manager` interface.

Generate a stub Dockerfile:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager update dnd --dockerfile-stub
Updating environment: dnd
Stub Dockerfile: ~/.config/morloc/environments/dnd/Dockerfile
Edit it, then run: morloc-manager update dnd
Environment 'dnd' updated.
----

Edit that file to install `jq` (a common JSON processing tool):

[source, dockerfile]
.~/.config/morloc/environments/dnd/Dockerfile
----
ARG CONTAINER_BASE=scratch
FROM ${CONTAINER_BASE}

RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y jq && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
----

The `CONTAINER_BASE` build arg is substituted with the environment's base
image at build time. Rebuild:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager update dnd
Updating environment: dnd
...
Environment 'dnd' updated.
----

Verify the new tool is available inside the container:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- jq --version
jq-1.7.1
----

This same mechanism works for any system package, Python library, R
package, or arbitrary Dockerfile instruction. A content hash on the
Dockerfile lets `update` skip the rebuild when nothing has changed.

The `jq` package is just a stand-in here -- the dice roller doesn't need
it. In a real project you would add whatever your code actually depends on
(e.g., `libcurl-dev`, a Python package via `pip install`, or an R package
via `install.packages()`).

==== Step 5: Install the program

Up to now, the compiled `dnd` binary has lived in your working directory.
To include it in the environment's permanent state -- so the nexus can
serve it, and so `freeze` can export it -- install it:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- morloc make --install dnd.loc
----

`--install` builds the binary and compiled pools directly into the environment's
permanent state, along with the files listed in `package.yaml`'s `include` field.
No build artifacts are left in the working directory.

==== Step 6: Serve it locally

With at least one installed program, you can serve the environment over
HTTP:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager start
Starting serve container morloc-serve-dnd on ports 8080:8080...
Container morloc-serve-dnd started
  Stop:   morloc-manager stop
  Status: morloc-manager status
----

`start` bind-mounts the environment's data directory into a container that
runs `morloc-nexus --router` as its entrypoint. No build step is needed --
the nexus reads the installed programs at startup.

Call the service:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ curl -s localhost:8080/health
{"status":"ok"}

$ curl -s localhost:8080/programs
{"programs":[{"name":"dnd","running":false,"commands":[{"name":"rollAdv","type":"remote","return_type":"Str"}]}]}

$ curl -s -X POST localhost:8080/call/dnd/rollAdv
{"status":"ok","result":"You rolled a 19"}
----

NOTE: The first request to a newly started service may take a few seconds
while language daemons initialize. Subsequent requests are fast.

And stop it:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager stop
Stopped serving environment: dnd
----

This is the simplest deployment path: your environment becomes a running
service whenever you want it. No image build, no registry push, no
Kubernetes manifest.

==== Step 7: Check environment health

Before deploying, you can check that the environment is properly set up
with the `doctor` command:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager doctor
Environment: dnd (local)
Engine:      podman

Prerequisites
  [ok] podman engine reachable (5.3.1)
  [ok] Base image ghcr.io/morloc-project/morloc/morloc-full:0.79.0
  [ok] Data directories intact

Manifests
  [ok] dnd -- built with 0.79.0, 3 pools

Deep checks
  [--] Use --deep to run container-side checks

5 passed, 0 warnings, 0 errors
----

`doctor` validates the environment in three areas:

* *Prerequisites*: Is the container engine reachable? Do the base and
  built images exist locally? Are the data directories intact?
* *Manifests*: Is each program manifest valid JSON? Does the
  `build.morloc_version` in each manifest match the environment's
  configured Morloc version? Do pool files exist?
* *Deep checks* (`--deep`): Runs inside the container. Confirms
  `morloc --version` matches expectations, and smoke-tests each installed
  program.

The `--deep` flag starts a container for each check, so it is slower but
catches issues that host-side checks cannot -- such as a missing shared
library or a broken Python import path:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager doctor --deep
...
Deep checks
  [ok] morloc 0.79.0 (matches config)
  [ok] dnd -- smoke test passed

7 passed, 0 warnings, 0 errors
----

If `doctor` reports a version mismatch in a manifest, recompile the
affected program:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- morloc make --install dnd.loc
----

==== Step 8: Export for external deployment

For deploying to a different machine, a Kubernetes cluster, or a container
registry, `morloc-manager` provides a freeze/unfreeze pipeline.

*Freeze* packages the environment's installed state as a portable tarball
plus an auditable manifest. Before creating any artifacts, `freeze`
validates that every installed program starts correctly:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager freeze -o ./dnd-freeze
Detecting morloc version from image...
Validating installed programs...
  [ok] dnd (1 commands)
Freezing installed state from ~/.local/share/morloc/environments/dnd...
Created ./dnd-freeze/state.tar.gz
Wrote ./dnd-freeze/freeze-manifest.json
Frozen state written to ./dnd-freeze
----

If any program fails validation (for example, a missing source file or
a broken import), `freeze` aborts before creating the tarball.

The output contains:

`state.tar.gz`:: The `lib/`, `fdb/`, `bin/`, `exe/`, `opt/`, and `src/`
directories -- everything needed to reproduce the environment at runtime.
`freeze-manifest.json`:: A record of the Morloc version, timestamp, installed
modules with SHA-256 checksums, compiled programs with their exported
commands, base image, and the Dockerfile layer used.

*Unfreeze* builds a standalone container image from that state and then
validates the result:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager unfreeze --from ./dnd-freeze/state.tar.gz --tag dnd-serve:v1
Using base image: ghcr.io/morloc-project/morloc/morloc-full:0.79.0
Extracting frozen state...
Built serve image: dnd-serve:v1
Validating installed programs...
  [ok] dnd (1 commands)
----

If post-build validation fails, the image is kept (for debugging) but
`unfreeze` exits with a non-zero status so scripts and CI pipelines
detect the failure.

The resulting image contains the morloc-nexus router, Morloc compiler,
language runtimes, and the frozen state. It runs anywhere a container
engine runs:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ podman run -d -p 8080:8080 dnd-serve:v1
$ curl -s -X POST localhost:8080/call/dnd/rollAdv
{"status":"ok","result":"You rolled a 14"}
----

Push it to a registry, deploy it to Kubernetes, or ship it to a collaborator.
The image is self-contained and does not need `morloc-manager`.

==== Recap

You went from nothing to a deployable multi-language service in nine
commands:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager new dnd --version 0.79.0            # create environment
$ morloc-manager select dnd                          # activate it
$ morloc-manager run -- morloc install root root-py root-r root-cpp
$ morloc-manager update dnd --dockerfile-stub        # add system deps
$ morloc-manager run -- morloc make --install dnd.loc # install program
$ morloc-manager start                               # serve locally
$ morloc-manager doctor --deep                       # validate health
$ morloc-manager freeze -o ./dnd-freeze              # export state
$ morloc-manager unfreeze --from ... --tag dnd-serve:v1  # build image
----

Along the way you saw:

* *Cross-language composition.* The same `rollAdv` composition worked
  unchanged whether `roll` was implemented in Python or R, and `max` was
  implemented in Python or {cpp}. Morloc's type system spans the languages,
  and the compiler generates the glue.
* *Environment isolation.* All dependencies lived in the `dnd` environment.
  You could create a second environment for another project without any
  interaction between the two.
* *Two deployment paths.* `start` gave you a local HTTP service in one
  command. `freeze`/`unfreeze` produced a portable image for external
  deployment. Both paths validate programs before proceeding.
* *Auditable packaging.* The freeze manifest records exactly which modules,
  programs, base image, and Dockerfile layer went into the frozen state.
  The serve image runs with a read-only filesystem, eliminating drift
  between what was tested and what is deployed.
* *Built-in diagnostics.* `doctor` checks environment health at a glance --
  from container engine connectivity to manifest version consistency --
  so you can diagnose issues before they reach production.

From here, you can compose `roll` with statistics functions from another
module, wrap a real machine-learning model in the same CLI+HTTP dual
interface, or chain multiple Morloc services together -- each one a typed
API surface discovered via the nexus's `/programs` and `/discover`
endpoints.

==== Appendix: System environment setup

System environments are shared across users on a machine. They require root
to create and configure, but allow non-root users to compile and run
programs. The full setup sequence is:

[%nowrap, console]
----
# 1. Create the system environment (as root)
$ sudo morloc-manager new shared --system --version 0.79.0

# 2. Activate it for root so the next `run` command targets it
$ sudo morloc-manager select shared --system

# 3. Install required modules (as root) -- users cannot do this step
$ sudo morloc-manager run -- morloc install root root-py root-r root-cpp

# 4. Add users to the container engine group (for docker)
$ sudo usermod -aG docker <username>

# 5. Each user selects the system environment
$ morloc-manager select shared
----

IMPORTANT: Step 2 is required because `new` does not auto-activate the
environment. Without it, Step 3 fails with "No active environment" for
root. Step 3 itself is also mandatory -- without it, regular users will
see `Permission denied` errors when compiling, because `morloc install`
creates directories under `/opt/morloc/` that only root can write.

NOTE: The `--system` flag in Step 2 writes to the system-wide config, which
serves as a default for users who have *no local active environment*. If
root (or any user) has previously run `morloc-manager select` without
`--system`, that local selection takes priority over the system default.
On non-fresh systems, run `sudo morloc-manager select shared` (without
`--system`) to update root's local active environment directly.

For podman setups, users do not need group membership -- podman runs
rootless by default. For docker setups, users must be in the `docker`
group.

NOTE: When running `sudo -u <user> morloc-manager ...` from a directory
that the target user cannot access (e.g., `/root`), the container engine
may fail with a misleading error. Run from `/tmp` or the target user's
home directory instead.
